# Plan - Polish Matrix Animations

The user found the matrix animations "jarring" and "blurry". We will remove the blur effect and overhaul the transitions to be smoother and more cinematic.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> I will be removing the blur effect entirely as it interferes with readability during transitions. I will also be changing the row swap logic to avoid "remounting" the matrix, which should eliminate flickering.

## Proposed Changes

### CSS Refinement
#### [MODIFY] [style.css](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/lectvideoanalyser/MathViz/src/style.css)
- Remove `.matrix-cell.changing .cell-value { filter: blur(2px); }`.
- Improve `.cell-value` transition timing with a custom quintic ease.
- Increase `.row-op-badge` and `.row-op-label` font sizes for better visibility.

### Matrix Animation Overhaul
#### [MODIFY] [matrix-renderer.ts](file:///Users/oli/Desktop/lectvideoanalyser/MathViz/src/renderers/matrix-renderer.ts)
- **Transform Animation**: Increase the `translateY` distance and the duration slightly for a more "Manim-like" feel.
- **Row Swap Logic**: Refactor `animateRowSwap` to update the existing DOM structure (moving `matrix-cell` elements or entire row wrappers) instead of re-mounting the whole matrix. This prevents layout jumps and flickers.
- **Elimination Animation**: 
  - Make the "ghost" elements more vibrant.
  - Slow down the "Arithmetic" display phase so the user has time to read the operation.
  - Use a clearer "scale-in" effect for the results.

### Engine Integration (if needed)
- Ensure `math-engine.ts` isn't sending too many redundant updates that cause animation overlap.

## Verification Plan

### Automated Tests
- I will use the `browser` tool to:
  - Play the "Matrix Row Reduction" diagnostic scene.
  - Verify that row swaps look like actual rows sliding past each other without flickering.
  - Verify that cell transformations are sharp (no blur) and use smooth easing.
  - Check that the "Ghost" cells in elimination steps follow a clear arc.

### Manual Verification
- Ask the user to confirm if the "blur" is gone and if the animations feel more fluid and less jarring.
